{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1408 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "b4d3bae2107692455105b13ad0c3da5bb6e7b9e487254a7c4b098db1d857c939" :edb/db-before-t 1407 :edb/db-after-t 1408 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534720 50 #inst "2026-10-08T05:03:15.678Z" 13194139534720 true] [17592186195307 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305382 13194139534720 true] [17592186195307 1104 #edb/ref 17592186305382 13194139534720 true] [17592186195308 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305381 13194139534720 true] [17592186195308 1104 #edb/ref 17592186305381 13194139534720 true] [17592186195342 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305384 13194139534720 true] [17592186195342 1104 #edb/ref 17592186305384 13194139534720 true] [17592186195343 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305383 13194139534720 true] [17592186195343 1104 #edb/ref 17592186305383 13194139534720 true] [17592186195944 1104 #edb/ref 17592186195947 13194139534720 true] [17592186196318 1104 #edb/ref 17592186196319 13194139534720 true] [17592186198078 1100 "Describe the set $S = \\{-9,-8,-7, ..., 5\\}$ using a conditional definition." 13194139534720 true] [17592186198078 1100 "Describe the set $S = \\{-9,-8,-7,..., 5\\}$ using a conditional definition." 13194139534720 false] [17592186198078 1145 "The set $S$ contains all integer numbers between $-9$ and $5$ (including $-9$ and $5$).\n\nTherefore, the set can be written as\n\n$$\nS = \\{ x \\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb Z} \\: : \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-9} \\leq x \\leq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5} \\}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: This definition is not unique. For example, we can write $S$ equivalently as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{ x \\in \\mathbb Z \\: : \\: -10 \\lt x \\lt 6 \\big\\}\n$$" 13194139534720 true] [17592186198078 1145 "The set $S$ contains all integer numbers between $-9$ and $5$ (including $-9$ and $5$).\n\nTherefore, the set can be written as\n\n$$\nS = \\{x \\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb Z} \\:: \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-9} \\leq x \\leq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5} \\}\n$$\n\n**Note**: This definition is not unique. For example, we can write $S$ equivalently as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{x \\in \\mathbb Z \\:: \\: -10 \\lt x \\lt 6 \\big\\}\n$$" 13194139534720 false] [17592186198092 1100 "Describe the set $S=\\left\\{\\ldots, -36, -6, -1, -\\dfrac1{6}, -\\dfrac1{36}, ...\\right\\}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take $a_1 = -\\dfrac16$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S.$*" 13194139534720 true] [17592186198092 1100 "Describe the set $S=\\left\\{\\ldots, -36, -6, -1, -\\dfrac1{6}, -\\dfrac1{36},...\\right\\}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take $a_1 = -\\dfrac16$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S$.*" 13194139534720 false] [17592186198092 1145 "Notice that the elements of our set\n\n$$\n\\ldots,\\quad -36, \\quad -6, \\quad -1, \\quad -\\dfrac1{6}, \\quad -\\dfrac1{36}, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform a two-way infinite geometric sequence with a common ratio\n\n$$\nr=\\dfrac16{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{-6}{-36}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{-1}{-6}) \\\\\n&= ((-\\frac{1}{6}))/((-1)) \\\\\n&= ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting\n\n$$\na_1=-\\dfrac16\n$$\n\nthe $n$th term $a_n$ of our sequence for $n\\in\\mathbb{Z}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= (-\\frac{1}{6}) \\cdot (\\frac{1}{6})^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= - (\\frac{1}{6}) \\cdot (\\frac{1}{6})^{-1} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{6})^{n} \\\\\n&= - (\\frac{1}{6})^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set using a constructive definition as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\left\\{-\\left(\\dfrac16\\right)^{n} \\:: \\: n\\in \\mathbb{Z}\\right\\}\n$$" 13194139534720 false] [17592186198092 1145 "Notice that the elements of our set\n\n$$\n\\ldots,\\quad -36, \\quad -6, \\quad -1, \\quad -\\dfrac1{6}, \\quad -\\dfrac1{36}, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform a two-way infinite geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=\\dfrac16{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =(\\frac{-6}{-36}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-1}{-6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{6})}{(-1)} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting  $a_1=-\\dfrac16,$ the $n$th term $a_n$ of our sequence for $n\\in\\mathbb{Z}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{6})\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{6})\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{-1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n} \\\\  & =-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set using a constructive definition as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\left\\{-\\left(\\dfrac16\\right)^{n} \\: : \\: n\\in \\mathbb{Z}\\right\\}\n$$" 13194139534720 true] [17592186198093 1100 "Find the missing coefficients $p$ and $q$ in the conditional definition of the set given below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\pm 3\\textrm{i}\\} = \\Big\\{z \\in \\mathbb{C} \\: : \\: 4z^2+pz+q = 0 \\Big\\}\n$$" 13194139534720 true] [17592186198093 1100 "Find the missing coefficients $p$ and $q$ in the constructive definition of the set given below.\n\n$\\{\\pm 3\\textrm{i}\\} = \\Big\\{z \\in \\mathbb{C} \\:: \\: 4z^2+pz+q = 0 \\Big\\}$" 13194139534720 false] [17592186198093 1145 "The given set contains the roots $z\\in\\mathbb C$ of some quadratic polynomial $f(z)$.\n\nSince the quadratic polynomial has roots\n\n$$\nz_1=-3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nz_2=3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=4$, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(z) &= a(z - z_{1})(z - z_{2}) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot (z - (-3\\textrm{i}))(z - (3\\textrm{i})) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot (z + 3\\textrm{i})(z - 3\\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot (z^{2} - (3\\textrm{i})^{2}) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot (z^{2} - 9\\textrm{i}^{2}) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot (z^{2} + 9) \\\\\n&= 4z^{2} + 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the given set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\pm 3\\textrm{i}\\} = \\Big\\{z \\in \\mathbb{C} \\:: \\: 4z^2+\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}z+\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}36} = 0 \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=0$ and $q=36$." 13194139534720 false] [17592186198093 1145 "The given set contains the roots $z\\in\\mathbb C$ of some quadratic polynomial $f(z).$\n\nSince the quadratic polynomial has roots $z_1=-3\\textrm{i}$ and $z_2=3\\textrm{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=4,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(z) & =a(z-{z}_{1})(z-{z}_{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (z-(-3\\text{i}))(z-(3\\text{i})) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (z+3\\text{i})(z-3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot ({z}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot ({z}^{2}-9{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot ({z}^{2}+9) \\\\  & =4{z}^{2}+36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the given set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\pm 3\\textrm{i}\\} = \\Big\\{z \\in \\mathbb{C} \\: : \\: 4z^2+\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}z+\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}36} = 0 \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=0$ and $q=36.$" 13194139534720 true] [17592186198094 1100 "Describe the set $S=\\{... , -5, -3, -1, 1, 3, ...\\}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take $a_1 = 1$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S.$*" 13194139534720 true] [17592186198094 1100 "Describe the set $S=\\{..., -5, -3, -1, 1, 3,...\\}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take $a_1 = 1$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S$.*" 13194139534720 false] [17592186198094 1145 "Notice that the elements of our set\n\n$$\n\\ldots, \\quad -5, \\quad -3, \\quad -1, \\quad 1, \\quad 3, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform a two-way infinite arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =2 \\\\  & =-3-(-5) \\\\  & =-1-(-3) \\\\  & =1-(-1) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting  $a_1=1,$ the $n$th term $a_n$ of our sequence for $n\\in\\mathbb{Z}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)\\cdot d \\\\  & =1+(n-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1+2n-2 \\\\  & =2n-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set using a constructive definition as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{2n-1 \\: : \\: n \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\big\\}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** \n\nThe constructive definition of a set is typically not unique. \n\nSince the elements of $A$ are generated by a *two-way* infinite arithmetic sequence, we could have picked *any* term in our sequence as $a_1$ when determining $a_n.$ We selected $a_1 = 1$ because this led to a nice, simple expression in our constructive definition." 13194139534720 true] [17592186198094 1145 "Notice that the elements of our set\n$\\ldots, \\quad -5, \\quad -3, \\quad -1, \\quad 1, \\quad 3, \\quad \\ldots$\nform a two-way infinite arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= 2 \\\\\n&=-3 - (-5) \\\\\n&=-1 - (-3) \\\\\n&= 1 - (-1) \\\\\n&= ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $a_1=1$, the $n$th term $a_n$ of our sequence for $n\\in\\mathbb{Z}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1) \\cdot d \\\\\n&= 1 + (n - 1) \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 1 + 2n - 2 \\\\\n&= 2n - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set using a constructive definition as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{2n-1 \\:: \\: n \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\big\\}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!**\n\nThe constructive definition of a set is typically not unique.\n\nSince the elements of $A$ are generated by a *two-way* infinite arithmetic sequence, we could have picked *any* term in our sequence as $a_1$ when determining $a_n$. We selected $a_1 = 1$ because this led to a nice, simple expression in our constructive definition." 13194139534720 false] [17592186198390 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305468 13194139534720 true] [17592186198390 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305487 13194139534720 true] [17592186198390 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305500 13194139534720 true] [17592186198390 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305513 13194139534720 true] [17592186198391 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305526 13194139534720 true] [17592186198391 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305536 13194139534720 true] [17592186198391 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305546 13194139534720 true] [17592186198391 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305556 13194139534720 true] [17592186198392 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305391 13194139534720 true] [17592186198392 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305401 13194139534720 true] [17592186198392 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305411 13194139534720 true] [17592186198392 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305421 13194139534720 true] [17592186198392 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305439 13194139534720 true] [17592186198393 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305449 13194139534720 true] [17592186198393 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305455 13194139534720 true] [17592186198393 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305461 13194139534720 true] [17592186198716 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534720 true] [17592186198716 1100 "Find the missing coefficients in the conditional definition of the set ${3,4}$ given below.\n\n${3,4}={n\\in Z\\,:\\,2{n}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$n\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$=0}$" 13194139534720 true] [17592186198716 1100 "Find the missing coefficients in the constructive definition of the set ${3, 4}$ given below.${3, 4} = {n ∈ Z: 2n^{2} -$\n{{field-1}}\n$n +$\n{{field-2}}\n$= 0}$" 13194139534720 false] [17592186198716 1145 "The given set contains the roots $n\\in Z$ of some quadratic polynomial $p(n).$\n\nSince the polynomial has roots ${n}_{1}=3$ and ${n}_{2}=4,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=2,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(n) & =a(n-{n}_{1})(n-{n}_{2}) \\\\  & =2(n-3)(n-4) \\\\  & =2({n}^{2}-7n+12) \\\\  & =2{n}^{2}-14n+24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the given set as follows:\n\n$$\n{3,4}={n\\in Z\\,:\\,2{n}^{2}-14n+24=0}\n$$" 13194139534720 true] [17592186198721 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534720 true] [17592186198721 1100 "Find the missing coefficients in the conditional definition of the set ${-1,\\frac{1}{3}}$ given below.\n\n${-1,\\frac{1}{3}}={q\\in Q\\,:\\,3{q}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}\n$q\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$=0}$" 13194139534720 true] [17592186198721 1100 "Find the missing coefficients in the constructive definition of the set ${-1, \\frac{1}{3}}$ given below.${-1, \\frac{1}{3}} = {q ∈ Q: 3q^{2} +$\n{{field-1}}\n$q -$\n{{field-2}}\n$= 0}$" 13194139534720 false] [17592186198721 1145 "The given set contains the roots $q\\in Q$ of some quadratic polynomial $p(q).$\n\nSince the quadratic polynomial has roots ${q}_{1}=-1$ and ${q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=3,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(q) & =a(q-{q}_{1})(q-{q}_{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (q-(-1))(q-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =3(q+1)(q-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =3({q}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}q-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =3{q}^{2}+2q-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the given set as follows:\n\n$$\n{-1,\\frac{1}{3}}={q\\in Q\\,:\\,3{q}^{2}+2q-1=0}\n$$" 13194139534720 true] [17592186198771 1017 #edb/ref 17592186195938 13194139534720 false] [17592186198771 1017 #edb/ref 17592186305385 13194139534720 true] [17592186198771 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534720 true] [17592186198771 1100 "Describe the set $S = {…, 3, 6, 12, 24, 48,...}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take $a_{1} = 6$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S$.*\n\n$2^{n}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" 13194139534720 false] [17592186198771 1100 "Describe the set $S={…,3,6,12,24,48,...}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take ${a}_{1}=6$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S.$*\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {3}^{n}\n$$" 13194139534720 true] [17592186198771 1145 "Notice that the elements of our set\n\n$$\n…,\\,3,\\,6,\\,12,\\,24,\\,48,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a two-way infinite geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{12} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting  ${a}_{1}=6,$ the $n$th term ${a}_{n}$ of our sequence for $n\\in Z$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =6\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & =6\\cdot {2}^{n}\\cdot {2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2}\\cdot {2}^{n} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set using a constructive definition as follows:\n\n$$\nS={3\\cdot {2}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in Z}\n$$" 13194139534720 true] [17592186198799 1017 #edb/ref 17592186196323 13194139534720 false] [17592186198799 1017 #edb/ref 17592186305481 13194139534720 true] [17592186198799 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534720 true] [17592186198799 1100 "Describe the set $S = {...,-4,-2, 0, 2, 4,...}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take $a_{1} = 2$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S$.*\n\n$2n$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" 13194139534720 false] [17592186198799 1100 "Describe the set $S={...,-4,-2,0,2,4,...}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take ${a}_{1}=2$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S.$*\n\n$$\n2n+1\n$$" 13194139534720 true] [17592186198799 1145 "Notice that the elements of our set\n\n$$\n…,\\,-4,\\,-2,\\,0,\\,2,\\,4,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a two-way infinite arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =2 \\\\  & =-2-(-4) \\\\  & =0-(-2) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting  ${a}_{1}=2,$ the $n$th term ${a}_{n}$ of our sequence for $n\\in Z$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)\\cdot d \\\\  & =2+(n-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2+2n-2 \\\\  & =2n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set using a constructive definition as follows:\n\n$$\nS={2n\\,:\\,n\\in Z}\n$$" 13194139534720 true] [17592186305381 1034 "14" 13194139534720 true] [17592186305381 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534720 true] [17592186305381 1111 #uuid "1de51858-6a53-5c48-be00-c31c73a12a57" 13194139534720 true] [17592186305382 1034 "24" 13194139534720 true] [17592186305382 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534720 true] [17592186305382 1111 #uuid "20d56ff6-031c-5c97-9829-c17b0fa0efa8" 13194139534720 true] [17592186305383 1034 "2" 13194139534720 true] [1